    "title": "DC-area puppies take the field in 2026 Puppy Bowl - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Ben Dennis ,\n2 hours ago\nALEXANDRIA, Va. ( DC News Now ) — While countless football fans will be glued to the NFL playoffs, families with four-legged companions adopted through a local animal rescue will be rooting for a different kind of athlete — their pups.\nFour dogs adopted through Operation Paws for Homes, a regional animal rescue serving Virginia, Maryland, D.C. and Southern Pennsylvania, are set to appear in this year’s annual Puppy Bowl, airing Sunday, Feb. 8 at 2 p.m. One of them is Twig, an energetic 8-month-old pup now living her best life in Alexandria, Va.\nAfter running through several “play calls” during filming in October 2025 in New York, Twig enjoyed some well-earned downtime when DC News Now visited her home on Wednesday.\n“We’re so excited. We’re so proud of her,” said Twig’s owner, and ‘pawrent’ Chandan Panguluri.\nPanguluri said she was surprised when Operation Paws for Homes reached out to see if Twig and she would be nominated for the televised event.\n“Twig is very photogenic, and she also loves frisbee and chasing things, and just loves people in general,” she said. “It was one day of filming but three days of puppy chaos.”\nOperation Paws for Homes holds pet adoption event in Fairfax Irene Skricki, a volunteer event coordinator for Operation Paws for Homes, said the Puppy Bowl comes with purpose.\n“For our rescue — and I think for probably all the rescues that participate — the hope is that it brings attention to the potential that you can get a great puppy from a rescue,” she said. “You don’t have to go to a breeder. You don’t have to go to a pet store.”\n(Photo courtesy of Jennifer Dunn with Operation Paws for Homes) Skricki said this year marks the 11th Puppy Bowl to include dogs adopted through Operation Paws for Homes. And Twig isn’t the only standout.\nDogs with special needs will also appear in this year’s game, including Paddy from Prince George’s County, who was born without eyes.\n“I think a lot of people feel that rescue dogs are damaged in some way — that there’s something wrong with them,” Skricki said. “That’s just not true.”\nWhile many shelters and rescues across the region have animals waiting for their forever homes, Panguluri said she’s especially grateful for the team that helped her find Twig.\n“They’re all just the most energetic and passionate people about animals, which is just so refreshing to be around,” she said.\nSome of the other pups from Operation Paws for Homes heading to the Puppy Bowl to compete are Rosie from Herndon, Va., Poppy from Brandywine, Md. and Ozzy from Freehold, N.J.\nRosie is ready to compete at the Puppy Bowl. (Photo courtesy of Warner Bros. Discovery) Ozzy knows he’ll win the hearts of all the viewers. (Photo courtesy of Warner Bros. Discovery) Poppy will make sure to dominate the field. (Photo courtesy of Warner Bros. Discovery) Operation Paws for Homes will host a Puppy Bowl watch party and adoption event in Manassas at the Ornery Beer Company Taproom on Feb. 8 from 2 p.m. to 4 p.m.\nCopyright 2026 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to DC News Now | DC, Virginia, Maryland News, Weather, Traffic, Sports Live.\n3",
